.TH GLANE 1 "2026-07-31" "bibliothecaire" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
glane \- harvest page ninety-two from many public-domain texts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B glane
[\fIlist\fR|\fIstatus\fR]
.br
.B glane status
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B glane fetch
[\fB\-\-count\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B glane extract
.br
.B glane list
.br
.B glane rule
.br
.B glane
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B glane
is the harvesting wing of
.BR bibliothecaire .
It gleans one page \- page ninety\-two \- from each of many public\-domain
texts, and leaves the harvest flat on disk for a human to arrange. It forms
no opinion about what it gathers, and it puts the pages in no order of its
own.
.PP
It is a sibling of
.BR cueille (1),
not a replacement for it, and the difference is worth stating because the two
look alike from a distance.
.B cueille
asks the open finding aids whether one named work is reachable and prints
their answers; it deliberately fetches no full text.
.B glane
does the opposite job on a different input: it takes no named work at all,
draws a bounded run of popular texts from one archive, and keeps a single
page out of each. One reports on a work you name; the other harvests from a
corpus you do not.
.SS The rule, stated rather than pretended
Plain text has no pages, so "page ninety\-two" is a
.I stated deterministic rule
and not a discovered fact. After the archive's boilerplate header and footer
are stripped, the remaining text is repaginated at 38 lines to the page \- a
typical printed line count \- and page ninety\-two is lines 3459 through 3496.
A text too short to reach that page is skipped, and the skip is recorded
rather than dropped, because the difference between the size of the corpus
and the size of the harvest is part of the harvest.
.PP
.B rule
prints this rule and the line range it produces, so the number a page rests
on can be read off the utility instead of taken from prose that may have
drifted from it.
.PP
The alternative rule \- the true page ninety\-two of a scanned book, from
page images \- is not implemented here. Asked for, it exits
.B 4
and says so; it is not silently substituted with this one.
.SS Subcommands
.TP
.B list
Print every subcommand and whether tooling backs it. Honours
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B status
Report what is on disk right now: texts held in the cache, pages harvested,
and texts fetched but skipped as too short. Reads; changes nothing.
.TP
.B fetch
Draw texts from the archive into the cache. Sequential, one at a time, with a
fixed delay before each, carrying a contact address, from one source only.
.B \-\-count
asks for a number; the per\-run ceiling holds regardless of what is asked.
A text already cached is not re\-fetched.
.TP
.B extract
Apply the rule to every cached text, writing one page per text, plus the
manifest that records each page's title, author, source and line count, and
every text skipped as too short. The cache is discardable and the manifest is
not: everything a later reader needs is carried into the manifest so that a
tree with no cache is still complete.
.TP
.B rule
Print the pagination rule and the line range it yields. Reads nothing.
.PP
.B fetch
and
.B extract
fail loud. A fetch that gathers nothing, or an extract that produces nothing,
exits nonzero and says which \- never exit 0 over an empty harvest, because an
empty harvest reported as a clean run is indistinguishable from a full one at
the exit code.
.SH THE ARRANGING
.B glane
harvests and stops.
.B It does not order, group, rank, or lay out what it gathers,
and no flag on this page turns that on. Arrangement is a human act and it is
the point of the work; a utility that arranged the pages would have automated
the one part that was never mechanisable. Laying the harvest out for that act
is
.BR accroche (1),
which also does not arrange it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-count \fIN\fR
With
.BR fetch :
how many texts to draw. Clamped down to the per\-run ceiling, never up.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output. Honoured by
.BR list .
Requested of any other subcommand it exits 4 rather than being dropped,
because a flag silently ignored is a caller lied to.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Results on standard output, commentary suppressed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage summary, including the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the utility name and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of flags; everything after is a positional argument.
.SH THE COST BOUNDARY
.B glane does not spend money.
No subcommand of it spends, so it carries no
.B \-\-summon
flag: passing one is a usage error naming the absence rather than a flag
quietly accepted.
.B \-\-help
answers "can this cost me anything?" with no, on its own, without reference
to this page.
.PP
It does spend something, and the page names it so the boundary is not
mistaken for free.
.B fetch
spends requests against a public archive that owes it nothing: at most forty
in a run, one at a time, with a delay before each, from a single source,
under a User\-Agent that says who is calling. These limits are properties of
the utility rather than of the caller \- there is no flag that widens any of
them, which is why there is no way to be impolite through this page.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept. A subcommand that found its work already done is one of
these.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong. An unknown subcommand, an unknown flag, and
.B \-\-summon
are each one of these.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: the tooling to keep this promise does not exist here.
.B \-\-json
asked of a subcommand that does not produce it exits this way, as does the
scanned\-page rule.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it ran with valid arguments and could not finish. A fetch that
gathered nothing and an extract that produced nothing are each this.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: it cannot read what it was asked to report on \- a missing
implementation, an unreadable tree. This is "I cannot see", never "nothing to
report", because an empty report from a blind run reads exactly like an
honest empty harvest.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GLANE_ROOT
The tree the harvest lives under. Defaults to the directory above this
utility. A test harness sets it so a run touches a scratch tree instead of
the real harvest.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/page92.py
The implementation every subcommand above runs.
.TP
.I cache/
Full texts as fetched. Discardable, and untracked for that reason.
.TP
.I pages/
One harvested page per text. This is the material.
.TP
.I pages/manifest.json
Title, author, source and line count for every page, and every text skipped
as too short. Written by
.BR extract ,
read by
.BR accroche (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
Name the utility:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ glane --version
glane (bashified)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read the rule off the utility rather than out of prose:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ glane rule
page 92 = lines 3459-3496, at 38 lines per page, after boilerplate is stripped
.fi
.RE
.PP
List the subcommands and whether tooling backs each:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ glane list
MECHANIZED    extract
MECHANIZED    fetch
MECHANIZED    list
MECHANIZED    rule
MECHANIZED    status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Authorise spending on a utility that cannot spend. The flag is refused by
name rather than accepted and ignored:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ glane --summon
glane: --summon: this utility never spends; the flag does not exist here
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR accroche (1),
.BR cueille (1),
.BR verse (1),
.BR wget (1),
.BR sed (1),
.BR split (1),
.BR test (1)
.PP
Lineage:
.BR wget (1)
in drawing a bounded list of named files politely and skipping what it
already holds;
.BR split (1)
in cutting a stream into fixed\-size pieces by a stated rule and taking one
of them;
.BR sed (1)
in addressing a line range rather than a page, which is what a page actually
is here;
.BR test (1)
in reserving 0 for success and giving distinct nonzero codes distinct
meanings.
.SH HISTORY
Written 2026\-07\-31, when the project named
.B quatre-vingt-douze
folded into
.BR bibliothecaire .
That project existed because a 2026\-07\-26 rename split it out of the
original library drop; folding it back in unwinds that split, on the ground
that harvesting from open archives is a library function. The pipeline it
carried ran as three subcommands of one script; naming them subcommands of a
verb is the change this page asks for.
.PP
The constraint the project carried travels with the page and is not
paraphrased away:
.I user judgment IS the product, and the merge must not quietly automate the
.IR arranging .
